    "created_at": "2022-01-08T07:19:40Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-01-08T07:19:52Z",
    "body": "> I have no doubt you have seen people who complain about some module like AWS showing them just an icon and not figuring how what it means\r\n\r\nSure, but I also don't think rendering something like \"{cloud emoji} None\" is any less confusing. For that matter, I'm not convinced that the user would be any less confused if they had a full version present--we occasionally get questions about python (with version) or git (with branch) modules showing up in home directories. In my mind, these cases are not the result of a module being shown without a version, but the result of a module appearing where the user doesn't expect it--version or no, that doesn't change.\r\n\r\nThat leaves the option of not showing anything at all, but I'm reluctant to go this way because it is, in general, much easier to explain why something *is* present rather than why it *isn't* present. It can already be [tricky to diagnose why certain modules aren't showing up](https://github.com/starship/starship/discussions/3395) (partially due to the fact that our module-level logs are a little incomplete).\r\n\r\nAt the very least, I'd want this to be gated behind a configuration flag, but adding another flag to every language module might be a little beyond what I'd be willing to do until the presets system is implemented.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
