    "created_at": "2022-10-22T11:12:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-10-22T11:12:27Z",
    "body": "I think the two best alternative solutions are showing only the icon (current behavior) or showing the icon with a short human-speaking info like ‘no toolset’ or ‘missing toolset’.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nStarship has configuration that determines which tools it checks for and with. As such, if a module is configured as active (not disabled and in the prompt format), then it should show if (at all/potentially) applicable.  \r\nIf Starship can determine that files of the toolset exist, but the toolset is not available for information inspection, not showing the icon seems ill-advised. The configuration says to inspect and display them after all.\r\n\r\nWhile this reasoning adds the complexity of Starship module state and logic, this complexity is inherent - from a Starship technology side, as well as a consequence of technical disconnect between files, Starship modules, and toolsets. We should not shy away from it when it can help users determine issues and follow their expectations more closely.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nShowing only the icon can be confusing, and I can imagine, very confusing to users not very tech-savy - about PATH, toolsets, environments in general or the machine they are using specifically.  \r\nI think providing information about the lack of a useable toolset where it could and supposedly should be used is very useful.\r\n\r\nIf Starship is configured to show a module I would expect the intention to be to also use the toolset, if available. Thus, I would expect a missing toolset to be an environment issue more likely than desired behavior.\r\n\r\nIf a missing toolset is desired (e.g. deliberate narrow (CLI) environment), I think a short text like ‘missing toolset’ is reasonable. It clarifies the environment state, and is not so long that it would be very disturbing or disrupting. While the text seems long, when comparing it to a version number `v1.20.3` it is only twice as long, and things like a git branch name or other modules with more than just one information easily regularly match this length. So it does not seem unreasonable, unreasonable long or different, to use a short text like that.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nWhile other text may be used, like ‘N/A’, ‘uninspectible’, etc, may be technically correct suggestions, they are not precise enough for a user to determine what it says, and where to go from there. For the user, the important differentiation is that in this environment the toolset is not usable, not found.\r\n\r\nSo IMO ‘missing toolset’ is the most precise, useful, and reasonable text to use.\r\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
